<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kalmar</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kalmar is a city on the south-east coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a>, facing the island of Öland across the Kalmar Strait. Its castle guarded the medieval border with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a>, and in 1397 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eric-of-pomerania">Eric of Pomerania</a> was crowned there as king of Denmark, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> and Sweden, founding the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalmar-union">Kalmar Union</a>. The city was fought over repeatedly, including in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalmar-war">Kalmar War</a> of 1611 to 1613.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kalmar.jpg" alt="Kalmar" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: L.G.foto, Kiwijo2000, ginandtonique, Sniper Zeta and Shrek_38 Collage by Moralist. · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kalmar_collage.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Kalmar lies in the south-east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baltic-sea">Baltic Sea</a>, in the province and cultural region of Småland. It is the seat of Kalmar Municipality and the capital of Kalmar County, and the main route to the island of Öland crosses the Öland Bridge beside it. From the thirteenth to the seventeenth centuries it was one of Sweden's most important cities. The oldest evidence of a town dates from the 11th century. Kalmar's oldest city seal, from between 1255 and 1267, is the oldest known city seal in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scandinavia">Scandinavia</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The first foundations of a castle were laid in the 12th century with a round tower for guard and lookout, and the tower was expanded in the 13th century. There Queen Margaret called an assembly of the heads of state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a>, and on 13 July 1397 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalmar-union">Kalmar Union</a> treaty was signed, creating a union that lasted until 1523. At the time the Scanian lands, the provinces of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/blekinge">Blekinge</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/halland">Halland</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scania">Scania</a>, belonged to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a>, so Kalmar's position near the border, together with its harbour and trade, drew it into several feuds.</p><p>Two events are independently called the Kalmar Bloodbath: in 1505, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-king-of-england">King John</a> had the mayor and city council executed, and in 1599, by order of Duke Charles, later King Charles IX of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a>. In the 1540s King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gustav-i-of-sweden">Gustav Vasa</a> and later his sons Erik XIV and John III rebuilt the castle as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> castle seen today. Kalmar became a diocese of the Church of Sweden in 1603 and the capital of the newly founded Kalmar County in 1634. The city suffered in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalmar-war">Kalmar War</a> of 1611–1613, which began with a Danish siege of the castle, and counted 22 sieges in all, the last during the Scanian War in the 1670s; the castle was never taken.</p><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-roskilde">Treaty of Roskilde</a> in 1658 the borders of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> moved further south and Kalmar's strategic importance faded. In 1689 the king set up his main naval base at Karlskrona, and Kalmar lost its place as one of the main military outposts. A new fortified town was built on Kvarnholmen around the mid-1600s on baroque lines. Kalmar Cathedral, designed by Nicodemus Tessin the Elder and begun in 1660, was interrupted several times, including during the Scanian War, and finished in 1703.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Kalmar had 41,388 inhabitants in 2020 and is the third largest urban area in Småland. The city has a university, parts of Linnaeus University, and its cathedral is a leading example of baroque classicism in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kalmar” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1397 her great-nephew Eric of Pomerania was crowned king of all three realms at <b>Kalmar</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/kalmar-union">Kalmar Union</a></li></ul></section>
